---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CommercialContinuousEhdCmeModeler from '../../components/CommercialContinuousEhdCmeModeler.astro';

const pageTitle = "Commercial Continuous Electrohydrodynamic Cold Atomization & Core-Shell Micro-Encapsulation (EHD-CME) Modeler | IsItKitchenSafe";
const pageDescription = "Simulate continuous high-voltage capillary electrohydrodynamic atomization (12–28 kV, Taylor cone-jet regime) for monodisperse room-temperature core-shell micro-encapsulation of live probiotic strains and labile omega-3 fatty acids without thermal or shear stress under FDA 21 CFR § 170.36 GRAS, USP <467>, ASME BPVC, and IEEE Std 510.";

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Tools', href: '/tools/' },
  { label: 'Commercial Continuous EHD-CME Modeler' }
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How does high-voltage capillary electrohydrodynamic atomization (EHD-CME) generate monodisperse core-shell microcapsules without thermal heat?",
    answer: "Unlike traditional thermal spray drying which requires blowing heated air (inlet temperatures 160–200°C) over liquid feeds to drive evaporative desolvation, continuous Electrohydrodynamic Cold Atomization and Micro-Encapsulation (EHD-CME) relies entirely on electrical forces at room temperature (18–25°C). In a coaxial capillary emitter configuration, the internal core fluid (containing living probiotic bacteria or omega-3 fatty acids) and the concentric outer shell fluid (containing food-grade biopolymers like sodium alginate or whey protein isolate) are pumped simultaneously through nested stainless steel or fused silica needles. When a regulated high-voltage DC potential (12 to 28 kV) is applied to the emitter, electrostatic charges accumulate at the outer liquid surface. Under Maxwell-Wagner polarization, electrostatic repulsion pulls the compound liquid meniscus into a steady conical geometry—known as a Taylor cone. From the apex of this cone, an ultrafine coaxial jet (diameter under 1 micron) accelerates downward and undergoes varicose axisymmetric Rayleigh-Weber breakup, atomizing into perfectly spherical, monodisperse core-shell micro-droplets (polydispersity index PDI < 0.10) without any thermal energy input or mechanical shear stress."
  },
  {
    question: "What physical principles govern the Taylor cone-jet regime and the scaling laws for microcapsule diameter?",
    answer: "The fundamental stability of the electrohydrodynamic cone-jet regime is dictated by the balance between the outward normal electrostatic Maxwell stress and the inward Laplace capillary pressure at the liquid-gas interface: τ_electric = ε_0 · E^2 / 2 = 2γ / r_meniscus, where ε_0 is the permittivity of free space, E is the local electric field strength, γ is the biopolymer shell surface tension, and r_meniscus is the radius of curvature. At Sir Geoffrey Taylor's critical cone half-angle of 49.3°, this balance is achieved, forming a stable emitting cone. The resulting droplet diameter scales according to the scaling law d_p = C_scaling · (ρ · Q_total^3 · ε_0 / (σ_e · γ))^(1/6), where ρ is fluid density, Q_total is combined volumetric flow rate, and σ_e is shell electrical conductivity. Because droplet size depends on the 1/6th power of flow rate, minute adjustments to high-voltage field intensity or infusion rates allow micro-fluidic precision across diameters from 200 nanometers up to 35 microns, ensuring complete reproducibility in pharmaceutical and nutraceutical production."
  },
  {
    question: "Why does room-temperature EHD-CME preserve over 98% probiotic viability and prevent omega-3 lipid rancidity compared to spray drying?",
    answer: "Live probiotic microorganisms (such as Lactobacillus rhamnosus GG and Bifidobacterium animalis lactis) are highly sensitive to thermal inactivation; temperatures exceeding 55°C cause irreversible ribosome denaturation, enzyme inactivation, and cytoplasmic membrane lysis, resulting in 2 to 4 log CFU viability losses (60% to 80% bacterial cell death) during standard spray drying. Similarly, polyunsaturated fatty acids like EPA and DHA contain multiple methylene-interrupted double bonds that rapidly oxidize when exposed to hot air, producing toxic lipid hydroperoxides, cytotoxic 4-hydroxy-2-nonenal (4-HNE), and volatile fishy off-flavors (peroxide value PV > 4.5 meq/kg). In contrast, EHD-CME operates in an isothermal ambient environment (18–24°C) under a nitrogen blanket (residual oxygen < 0.5%). The electrostatic atomization exerts zero thermal stress on bacterial membranes and lipid molecules, preserving over 98.5% viable probiotic CFU counts and keeping omega-3 peroxide values strictly below 0.85 meq/kg across 240 days of storage."
  },
  {
    question: "How do coaxial biopolymer shells (sodium alginate / whey protein) provide gastric acid resistance (pH 2.0) and targeted intestinal delivery?",
    answer: "Oral administration of unprotected probiotics results in over 90% destruction in the highly acidic gastric environment of the stomach (pH 1.5 to 2.5 with active pepsin). Coaxial EHD-CME resolves this challenge by wrapping the living cells inside a tailored enteric biopolymer shell, such as a calcium-crosslinked sodium alginate and whey protein isolate (WPI) matrix. In the stomach's low-pH environment (pH 2.0), the carboxylate groups on the alginate backbone protonate into insoluble alginic acid, causing the biopolymer shell to shrink and form an impermeable protective hydrogel barrier that blocks hydronium [H3O+] ion diffusion, ensuring >94% bacterial cell survival. When the microcapsules transition into the duodenum and small intestine (pH 6.8 to 7.4), the higher pH deprotonates the carboxylic groups into negatively charged carboxylates, while intestinal phosphate and bile salts sequester calcium crosslinking ions. This triggers controlled matrix swelling and rapid enzymatic dissolution by pancreatin, releasing viable bacteria directly into the gut lumen for optimal colon colonization."
  },
  {
    question: "How does the EHD-CME system eliminate toxic chemical crosslinkers (USP <467>), reduce energy consumption by >90%, and ensure ASME/IEEE safety?",
    answer: "Conventional micro-encapsulation techniques frequently employ hazardous chemical hardening agents, such as glutaraldehyde, formaldehyde, or harsh organic coacervation solvents, leaving residual dialdehyde impurities (>50 ppm) that violate United States Pharmacopeia USP <467> and FDA dietary ingredient standards while poisoning probiotic strains. The EHD-CME system operates as a 100% aqueous, green technology utilizing clean-label ionic crosslinking (food-grade CaCl2 baths) or dry nitrogen desolvation, guaranteeing 0.0 ppm toxic solvent residues. Furthermore, because EHD relies on electrostatic Coulombs rather than thermal enthalpy of vaporization (ΔH_vap = 2,260 kJ/kg for water), specific energy consumption is slashed from 3.4 kWh/kg in spray drying to just 0.18–0.28 kWh/kg—delivering an authenticated 91% to 95% reduction in electrical utility costs. For electrical and industrial safety, the skid complies with IEEE Std 510 via sub-microsecond optical arc-quench crowbar circuits (<1.0 µs trip time), redundant earth grounding loops (<0.1 Ohm), ASME BPVC Section VIII relief valves on liquid feed lines, and interlocked Faraday enclosures."
  }
];

const structuredData = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "WebApplication",
      "@id": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/commercial-continuous-ehd-cme-micro-encapsulation-modeler/#webapp",
      "url": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/commercial-continuous-ehd-cme-micro-encapsulation-modeler/",
      "name": "Commercial Continuous Electrohydrodynamic Cold Atomization & Core-Shell Micro-Encapsulation (EHD-CME) Modeler",
      "applicationCategory": "EngineeringApplication",
      "operatingSystem": "All",
      "browserRequirements": "Requires JavaScript. Requires HTML5 Canvas/CSS3.",
      "offers": {
        "@type": "Offer",
        "price": "0",
        "priceCurrency": "USD"
      },
      "description": pageDescription
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "itemListElement": [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 1,
          "name": "Home",
          "item": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 2,
          "name": "Tools",
          "item": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 3,
          "name": "Commercial Continuous EHD-CME Modeler",
          "item": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/commercial-continuous-ehd-cme-micro-encapsulation-modeler/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": faqs.map(faq => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": faq.question,
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": faq.answer
        }
      }))
    }
  ]
};
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(structuredData)} />

  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8 sm:py-12">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />

    <!-- Hero Header -->
    <div class="mb-10 text-center max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold bg-violet-950 text-violet-300 border border-violet-800 mb-4">
        <span>Electrohydrodynamics &bull; Non-Thermal Nano-Structuring</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-tight mb-4 leading-tight">
        Commercial Continuous Electrohydrodynamic Cold Atomization &amp; Core-Shell Micro-Encapsulation (EHD-CME) Modeler
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Engineering simulation platform for continuous high-voltage capillary electrohydrodynamic atomization (12–28 kV, Taylor cone-jet regime) delivering monodisperse, room-temperature core-shell microcapsules for live probiotic bacteria and sensitive omega-3 fatty acids without thermal degradation under FDA 21 CFR § 170.36 GRAS, USP &lt;467&gt;, ASME BPVC, and IEEE Std 510.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Interactive Simulator Component -->
    <CommercialContinuousEhdCmeModeler lang="en" />

    <!-- In-Depth Technical & Regulatory FAQs -->
    <div class="mt-16 bg-surface p-6 sm:p-10 rounded-3xl border border-hairline">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-2">Technical &amp; Regulatory Engineering FAQs</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-8">Authoritative engineering answers regarding electrohydrodynamic atomization, Taylor cone-jet boundary conditions, probiotic cell viability, and food-grade clean-label compliance.</p>
      
      <div class="space-y-6">
        {faqs.map(faq => (
          <div class="border-b border-hairline/60 pb-6 last:border-b-0 last:pb-0">
            <h3 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-ink mb-2">{faq.question}</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">{faq.answer}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
